 ],
 "literal": "To the calf of the wild cow caught in the vicinity of a tiger / an old cow whose grief has passed, offering her teat, nurses / in the forest ... to / yielding what is desired, unstinting generosity / if the quiver of bright spears is like that, the bright sword / except for the pillar-footed elephant / does not know being drawn from its sheath, such is the town of the spear-bearing warrior.",
 "translation": "In the forest, an old domestic cow whose grief has passed nurses the calf of a wild cow that was killed by a tiger, by offering her own teat. In such a place, if his quiver of bright spears is like unstinting generosity that yields whatever is desired, his bright sword is never drawn from its sheath except to strike down pillar-legged elephants. Such is the town of this spear-bearing warrior.",
 "modern": "புலியிடம் அகப்பட்டுக்கொண்ட காட்டுப் பசுவின் கன்றுக்கு, தன் கன்றை இழந்த துயரம் நீங்கிய வீட்டுப் பசுவானது தன் மடியைச் சேர்த்துப் பால் புகட்டும் காட்டை உடையது அந்த ஊர். வேண்டியதை எல்லாம் வாரி வழங்கும் வள்ளல் தன்மை கொண்ட வெண்மையான வேல்களைக் கொண்ட அம்பறாத்தூணியை உடையவன் அந்த வீரன். அவனது ஒளிரும் வாளானது, உரல் போன்ற கால்களை உடைய யானைகளை வீழ்த்துவதற்கே அன்றி, பிறவற்றிற்காக உறையிலிருந்து வெளிப்பட அறியாது. அத்தகைய வேலேந்திய வீரனின் ஊர் இதுவாகும்.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Vākai tiṇai and Vallāṇ Mullai tuṟai, which celebrates the strength, valor, and domestic virtue of a warrior in the forest region. The poet describes the warrior's town and his exceptional valor. The opening image shows an old domestic cow nursing an orphaned wild cow calf whose mother was killed by a tiger. This image of natural compassion and abundance sets the tone for the warrior's own character. The warrior's quiver is filled with bright spears that are always ready to be given like unstinting generosity that yields whatever is desired. His bright sword is so noble and powerful that it is never drawn from its sheath for minor foes, but only to face mighty, pillar-legged enemy elephants.",
